Id: lin052w
Severity: WARN
Category: network
Check: check_network_config
Fix: Set net.ipv4.conf.all.proxy_arp=0 in /etc/sysctl.d; an interface that must proxy takes conf/IFACE/proxy_arp=1 alone.
References: Linux kernel networking documentation (ip-sysctl, proxy_arp); RFC 1027
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST SC-5, SC-7; ISO 8.20; CE Secure configuration

Proxy ARP is on for every interface (net.ipv4.conf.all.proxy_arp is
1): the host answers ARP requests for addresses that are not its own
but that it has a route to, so its neighbours send that traffic to it.
That is a router's job (RFC 1027), and done for one interface at a
time: a VPN server or a container bridge sets conf/IFACE/proxy_arp on
the interface concerned. On conf/all the host claims, on every network
it is on, every address it can route, which draws its neighbours'
traffic through it and lets a misrouted or malicious host sit between
them. The kernel's default is 0 and no distribution changes it.
  # sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.all.proxy_arp=0
